o as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97751,"python_rank":16276},{"name":"davidbmar/Palindrome-Index","owner":"davidbmar","html_url":"https://github.com/davidbmar/Palindrome-Index","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Given a string, , of lowercase letters, determine the index of the character whose removal will make  a palindrome. If  is already a palindrome or no such character exists, then print . There will always be a valid solution, and any correct answer is acceptable. For example, if  \"bcbc\", we can either remove 'b' at index  or 'c' at index .  Input Format  The first line contains an integer, , denoting the number of test cases.  Each line  of the  subsequent lines (where ) describes a test case in the form of a single string, .  Constraints  All characters are lowercase English letters. Output Format  Print an integer denoting the zero-indexed position of the character that makes  not a palindrome; if  is already a palindrome or no such character exists, print .  Sample Input  3 aaab baa aaa Sample Output  3 0 -1 Explanation  Test Case 1: \"aaab\"  Removing 'b' at index  results in a palindrome, so we print  on a new line.  Test Case 2: \"baa\"  Removing 'b' at index  results in a palindrome, so we print  on a new line.  Test Case 3: \"aaa\"  This string is already a palindrome, so we print ; however, , , and  are also all acceptable answers, as the string will still be a palindrome if any one of the characters at those indices are removed.","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97766,"python_rank":16277},{"name":"halfak/python-para","owner":"halfak","html_url":"https://github.com/halfak/python-para","stars":1,"forks":1,"watchers":1,"issues":1,"language":"Python","description":"a set utilities that ake advantage of python's `multiprocessing` module to distribute CPU-intensive tasks","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97769,"python_rank":16278},{"name":"jSith/GroupBot","owner":"jSith","html_url":"https://github.com/jSith/GroupBot","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":2,"issues":5,"language":"Python","description":"Groupme Bots","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97771,"python_rank":16279},{"name":"sseshan7/telemetry-processing","owner":"sseshan7","html_url":"https://github.com/sseshan7/telemetry-processing","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":3,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Desktop side code to process, store, and visualize IMU data","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97774,"python_rank":16280},{"name":"milton0825/cpython-internals","owner":"milton0825","html_url":"https://github.com/milton0825/cpython-internals","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97775,"python_rank":16281},{"name":"anican/generalization","owner":"anican","html_url":"https://github.com/anican/generalization","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":2,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"experiment pipeline for pdgl challenge","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97793,"python_rank":16282},{"name":"mulargui/kaggle-Classify-forest-types-MLOps","owner":"mulargui","html_url":"https://github.com/mulargui/kaggle-Classify-forest-types-MLOps","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"end to end deployment of a neural model in Prod","topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97800,"python_rank":16283},{"name":"petsan/bug-free-sniffle","owner":"petsan","html_url":"https://github.com/petsan/bug-free-sniffle","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":2299,"global_rank":97809,"python_rank":16284},{"name":"petsan/automatic-octo-robot","owner":"petsan","html_url":"https://github.com/petsan/automatic-octo-robot","stars":